==Abstract==
This draft defines how an independently deployed Neo N3 contract declares its SmartAccount module role and how an account core calls it. A basic account needs no modules to receive or transfer assets. This document and the companion [[nep-smartaccount-module-profiles.mediawiki|Module Profiles]] are local, unnumbered proposals. They do not claim network activation.

==Motivation==
A wallet needs to distinguish a transaction validator, a policy hook and a recovery seat before installation. A module author needs the exact ABI, byte representation and authority of each callback. Neither a display name nor a manifest entry alone supplies these guarantees.

==Specification==
MUST, MUST NOT and MAY describe requirements of this draft. Types below are Neo manifest types; parameter order, arity, case-sensitive method name and return type are normative. The [../SA-MODULE-PROTOCOL.md SmartAccount protocol] defines account identity, operation fields, digests and execution phases; this proposal adds no alternate operation encoding.

===Discovery and encoding===
Every module MUST expose safe <code>getModuleProfile(): String</code>. The result MUST be a UTF-8 JSON object of 1 to 2048 bytes, without BOM, duplicate keys, whitespace between tokens or a trailing newline. Object keys MUST be sorted lexicographically, recursively. Strings MUST use the short escapes <code>\"</code>, <code>\\</code>, <code>\b</code>, <code>\t</code>, <code>\n</code>, <code>\f</code> and <code>\r</code> for their respective characters. Other U+0000 through U+001F controls use <code>\u00xx</code> with lowercase hexadecimal digits. All remaining characters, including slash and non-ASCII names, use their literal UTF-8 encoding; the defined field names, capability names and callback names are ASCII. Arrays retain the order specified below. A digest is SHA-256 of the returned UTF-8 bytes, not of RPC base64, NEF bytes, a parsed object or an envelope.

{| class="wikitable"
! Field !! Required value
|-
| profileVersion || Integer 1; identifies the profile grammar, not a product release.
|-
| contractName || String equal to the deployed manifest name; empty names and names consisting entirely of Unicode White_Space characters are invalid.
|-
| moduleType || One of <code>validator</code> (installation code 1), <code>hook</code> (4), <code>recovery</code> (5).
|-
| lifecycle || <code>["onInstall","onUninstall"]</code>; append <code>"configure"</code> if and only if the manifest exposes <code>configure/4</code>. That method MUST match the callback signature below. A method of the same name with a different arity does not implement this callback.
|-
| callbacks || Validator: <code>["validateUserOp","onUserOpComplete"]</code>, append <code>"isValidSignature"</code> when message capability is enabled and <code>"planHooks"</code> when the planner seat is declared. Hook: <code>["preCheck","postCheck"]</code>. Recovery: <code>[]</code>.
|-
| capabilities || Object with four required fields: <code>instanceIsolation</code>, <code>messageCapability</code>, <code>thresholdMemberEligible</code> (Booleans) and <code>compositeLayer</code> (integer 0 or 1). Two optional seat fields, <code>governance</code> and <code>hookPlanner</code>, MUST be the Boolean <code>true</code> when present and MUST be omitted when false; only validators may declare them. The core accepts configuration landing, module administration and an ordinary thaw only when the calling contract is the installed validator instance that declares <code>governance</code>; the delay before landing is module state. It calls <code>planHooks</code> on the single instance declaring <code>hookPlanner</code>. Only validators may declare message or member capability. Eligibility alone never admits a key or member to a mandatory spending policy.
|}

The [[smartaccount-module-profile.schema.json|discovery JSON Schema]] checks the shape of the parsed object, including role-dependent callbacks, capabilities and lifecycle order. An editor can use it without contacting a node. Do not add a schema URL to the profile itself. A JSON Schema cannot distinguish duplicate keys discarded by the parser, measure the canonical bytes returned by the getter, or establish that the manifest and installation role match.

No other top-level fields are defined. A conforming publisher MUST supply all six fields. An implementation claiming instance isolation MUST separate mutable authority and accounting state by account and instance; other implementations MUST NOT require the core to install multiple instances of the same contract for one account. A composite layer declaration does not grant recursion rights.

===Callback ABI===
<pre>
all       getModuleProfile() -> String [safe]
all       onInstall(Hash160,Integer,Integer,Array) -> Void
all       onUninstall(Hash160,Integer,Integer,Array) -> Void
optional  configure(Hash160,Integer,Integer,Array) -> Void
validator validateUserOp(Hash160,ByteArray,Array) -> Boolean [safe]
validator onUserOpComplete(Hash160,Array,Any) -> Void
message   isValidSignature(Hash160,Hash160,Integer,Integer,ByteArray,Array) -> Boolean [safe]
validator planHooks(Hash160,ByteArray,Array) -> Array [safe]
hook      preCheck(Hash160,Array) -> ByteArray
hook      postCheck(Hash160,Array,Any,ByteArray) -> Void
</pre>
Lifecycle arguments dispatched by the core are account ID, instance ID, type code and data array. Configuration commands and any further interpretation of their fields belong to the named profile. A module MUST NOT trust an ID or generation supplied by a wallet as proof of callback authority. A named profile MAY expose management and read methods in addition to this interface.

Validation receives the account ID, 32-byte operation digest and operation array. Only Boolean true approves. Preview MUST NOT consume the nonce or write validator state. Completion receives the account ID, operation and target result. The optional message callback receives the account ID, original caller, instance ID, generation, message digest and payload. The core never dispatches it: applications call the module contract directly, as §8 of the protocol specifies. A spending grant MUST NOT silently authorize arbitrary messages.

Hooks run in the account's execution order before the target and in reverse order afterwards. Each post-check receives only its own pre-check bytes (maximum 512) along with the operation and target result. A rejected or faulting required callback MUST NOT yield a successful operation. Installation and execution MUST bind the actual deployment and its code revision. State-changing callbacks MUST authenticate their caller and applicable phase/instance. Recovery reset invalidates installation records without invoking uninstall callbacks: leftover module storage MUST NOT confer authority.

===Example===
For a native account, governance action <code>[3, moduleId, configData]</code> requests configuration. The reviewed governance contract submits the confirmed action to the core. The core authenticates governance, resolves the installed instance, checks its callback ABI and code binding, and calls <code>configure(accountId,moduleId,moduleType,configData)</code> in the configuration phase with <code>CallFlags.All</code>. The module interprets the command array. An ordinary custody witness alone cannot dispatch this action. Failure rolls back the whole group. Modules which bind credentials to a generation MUST read that generation from the authenticated instance descriptor.

The following bytes, without a final newline, are the complete profile for a recovery seat:
<pre>
{"callbacks":[],"capabilities":{"compositeLayer":0,"instanceIsolation":false,"messageCapability":false,"thresholdMemberEligible":false},"contractName":"RecoveryModule","lifecycle":["onInstall","onUninstall","configure"],"moduleType":"recovery","profileVersion":1}
</pre>

==Rationale==
One discovery call and three role codes suffice. A governance or read adapter can implement the validator ABI while always refusing spending; the companion profile states that behavior explicitly. Optional policies stay outside the basic transfer path.

==Backwards Compatibility==
This is the single unpublished SmartAccount module format. No legacy role aliases, previous ABI, or migration procedure is specified.

==Test Cases==
The [[../../tests/fixtures/sa-module-protocol/module-profile-vectors.json|portable discovery vectors]] supply the manifest name, installation role, exact profile text, SHA-256 and expected acceptance for independent implementations. Encode the decoded <code>profileText</code> field as UTF-8 without appending a newline; do not hash the surrounding fixture JSON. Each digest identifies the input, including for rejected inputs. Acceptance here is limited to discovery bytes and their declared identity.

Accept the recovery example with its matching manifest. Reject a changed name, duplicate capability, wrong type code, missing post-check, non-safe validator or an advertised message method returning ByteArray instead of Boolean. A conforming implementation must also satisfy the role's behavioral tests: manifest validation alone cannot prove authorization or rollback.

==Implementation==
See <code>SmartAccountModulePolicy.cs</code> for native admission. At installation the core checks the getter bytes, the role and capability declarations, the exact lifecycle and callback lists, and the corresponding manifest method signatures. The publisher can run <code>scripts/check_module_neps.py</code> before installation. Neither check proves the module enforces its spending or recovery rules. No official NEP number has been assigned.
